A||N||7||87ECO5||Simple notchback sail panel modification||||14||
Z||000000||87ECO5||08-28-2000||10:47 PM||||For those of you interested in adding a personal touch to your Fiero and, at the same time, ridding your car of those (IMHO) hideous Pontiac "arrowhead" emblems on the notchback sail panels, here are the simple steps I took to update and improve the appearance of my car.<P>Before starting this project, I had to decide what I wanted to put in place of the arrowheads.  I contacted Paul McKibben and explained the situation.  I told him I wanted a Pegasus but I only wanted it large enough to fit on the frame rail - not the actual sail panel.  After deciding on a size approx. 2" by 2", Paul said he'd play around with it and get back with me.  The results of his fine work are on the pages of this thread.<P>The next step was to remove the panels from the body and remove the emblems from their inserts.  On my car, one of the emblems was already missing so my work on this step was already 50% complete.  The other emblem came out with a small flathead screwdriver and light tapping:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87ECO5_sailmod_1.jpg">  <P>Once the emblems were removed, it was time to head to the workbench with the panels in one hand and a vibrating sander in the other.  Using 100 grit sandpaper, I carefully worked the sander (mostly in the area where the emblem was) until the entire surface was smooth:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87ECO5_sailmod_2.jpg">  <P>After filling in the holes with body filler, I went back over them with 220 and 400 grit to ensure a smooth surface for the paint.<P>After the sanding was complete, I prepared the panels for painting.  I had the option of removing the sail panel from the frame but I'd heard it's almost impossible to salvage the panel once this is done.  So, I used black electrical tape and notebook paper to mask off the panels:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87ECO5_sailmod_3.jpg">  <P>The paint I used was engine enamel high-gloss black.  I chose this type of paint because (a) it's made to withstand heat (something these panels must endure when they're in the sun), (b) it's durable, (c) it dries fast and (d) it provides a really nice finish compared to other gloss black paints like the Krylons and Rustoleums.<P>Once the paint was dry, I returned the panels to their positions on the body and applied Paul's decals:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87ECO5_sailmod_4.jpg">  <P>Here's a close-up of that decal:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87ECO5_pegasus_1.jpg">  <P>For $10, Paul sent me three of these little guys (the third being a successful test run that he threw in - no charge).  Not wanting the third decal to go to waste, I decided to use it on my new bra ... to sort of pull everything together:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87ECO5_360_wannabe_2_1.jpg">  <P>The entire project ran me about $20.  That includes the decals from Paul, the engine enamel paint and the electrical tape.<P>A word of caution:  Care must be taken when washing the panels because the very tiny points on the decals can be easily lifted by a sponge or towel.  I am planning to go back and clearcoat the frames.  This will both protect the decals from being lifted and will also protect the decals and paint from fading.<P>Finally ... I <B>HIGHLY</B> recommend Paul McKibben!  He is very professional, extremely talented, and a gem to deal with!  You will not find a better decal person!<P>Hope someone finds this helpful.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 08-28-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000001||theogre||08-28-2000||11:04 PM||||Nice job. I'll keep this on in mind if I ever get around to painting..... (I'll just be happy to get the power windows finished right now.)<P>Before you clear coat, get a scrap peice of the decal material and shoot it with the clear coat you are going to use. You don't want to eat up the new decals if the paint hates them. I've seen nasty things happen to decals that get painted.||||reg||1
Z||000002||pollock||08-29-2000||02:35 PM||||Good job, man.  Those really look nice.||||reg||1
